Actually, I use stethoscopes frequently in the Mines to spot which dwarf
has the mithril, so as to avoid early-game alignment futzing.  :)
Beyond that, no, you don't get the weapons at all, of course.

>> but I'm not
>> necessarily opposed to the _weapon_ being visible in this manner. 
> 
> Why do you think it might be bad to know what type of armor a
> creature wears (not the enchantments as I said, neither the AC)?

So you know what peacefuls to kill and what attacks to use.  Knowing
whether a priest has a CoMR or a robe, for example, is a key piece of
information when you're deciding whether to wand-of-death him, since
it'll make him angry... and it could be used to identify CoMR without
having to have one on hand, for that matter.

The weapon itself is far less critical, though; the number of weapons
that would significantly change the approach a player takes to a monster
is rather low and may well consist of "Vorpal Blade".

>> add it to the pile for consideration though; this is likely to be more
>> complex than it appears at first glance.
> 
> Curious; why do you think it might be complex?

Experience.  I've learned over time to assume that things in portions of
the code I haven't looked at before are complex, and then be pleasantly
surprised if they're not.  :)  Especially in something like NH, where
there are a great variety of interactions... see above for how this
could be used to identify CoMR for one example.

